{"step_index":37,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-20T23:04:01Z","content":"Here is the comparison and \"benchmark\" breakdown of **Gemini 3.1 Pro (High)** vs. **Claude Opus 4.6 (Thinking)** inside the Antigravity environment, based on their optimized use cases and structural strengths:\n\n### 1. Claude Opus 4.6 (Thinking) \n*The High-Reasoning & Architecture Model*\n\nOpus 4.6 is built for deep, extended thinking. It parses complex logic natively and is your go-to for heavy engineering tasks.\n* **Core Strengths:** Complex multi-file code generation, architecture design, deep debugging, and multi-step reasoning under uncertainty.\n* **How it Thinks:** It has built-in extended reasoning. It doesn't need heavy \"step-by-step\" hand-holding (in fact, over-prescribing Chain-of-Thought actually degrades its output). It parses XML tags natively, which drastically reduces hallucination on complex prompts.\n* **The \"Format Tax\":** If you ask Opus to do heavy reasoning *and* strict formatting at the exact same time, quality drops. It's best to ask it to reason first, then format the output.\n* **Best Used For:** \n  - Complex code generation across multiple files\n  - Tracing logic for root-cause bug analysis\n  - Designing system architecture and handling conflicting constraints\n\n### 2. Gemini 3.1 Pro (High)\n*The Long-Context Powerhouse*\n\nGemini 3.1 Pro is built for massive data ingestion (1M+ tokens) and speed. It is highly optimized for research and exploration.\n* **Core Strengths:** Fast execution, massive file reading, codebase-wide exploration, research, and documentation.\n* **How it Thinks:** It features tiered reasoning (Low/Medium/High). On the \"High\" setting, its native reasoning is very strong, but it can still suffer from the \"lost in the middle\" effect on massive prompts (always put your most critical instructions at the very beginning and very end). \n* **Formatting:** Gemini excels with JSON schemas 